    <h2>Protocol: How Instruction Compliance Is Measured</h2>

    <p>This benchmark evaluates whether language models follow a structured, multi-step protocol when given explicit procedural instructions. The protocol is deliberately designed to produce <strong>verifiable artifacts</strong> at each step, tool calls, file reads, structured citations, that an evaluation harness can audit without relying on the model&rsquo;s self-report.</p>

    <p>The underlying task is straightforward: a library of domain-specific rules (coding conventions, deployment checklists, security policies) is maintained independently of any model&rsquo;s training data. Models must consult the <em>current</em> version of relevant rules, not their parametric memory of what the rules used to say, before responding. The protocol enforces this by requiring a tool-call read of the actual file before the model may claim it as loaded.</p>

    <h3>The Protocol Sequence</h3>

    <p>On every user prompt, the system executes the following sequence:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Automatic Injection (Hook).</strong> A shell hook runs before the model sees the prompt. It extracts keywords, file extensions, and paths from the user&rsquo;s message, matches them against rule frontmatter, and injects two things into the model&rsquo;s context as a <code>&lt;system-reminder&gt;</code> block:
        <ul>
          <li>A compressed <strong>micro-kernel</strong> (~500 tokens) defining mandatory behaviors, validation sequences, and the rule-loading contract.</li>
          <li>A <strong>ranked candidate list</strong> of domain rules whose triggers matched the prompt content.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Selection &amp; Reading.</strong> The model selects the most relevant candidates from the injected list (up to 3), then reads each via the <code>read_file</code> tool. This is the critical grounding step; rule content is now in the model&rsquo;s context window, not just its parametric memory.</li>
      <li><strong>Citation.</strong> The model declares what it read in a structured PRE-FLIGHT header with version-tagged citations. This creates the auditable artifact.</li>
      <li><strong>Response.</strong> The model generates its response, informed by the loaded rules.</li>
    </ol>

