    <h3>The &ldquo;Read Before You Cite&rdquo; Contract</h3>
    <p>The protocol is explicit about what constitutes a &ldquo;loaded&rdquo; rule. Two conditions must both be met:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><code>read_file</code> was called on the rule and returned successfully (verifiable in the tool trace)</li>
      <li>The rule is declared in the PRE-FLIGHT header with a version tag</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This is the central invariant that the evaluation harness checks. When a model claims a rule is loaded (condition 2) without a corresponding <code>read_file</code> call (condition 1) in the tool trace, that is a protocol violation, specifically, failure mode FM-1 (<em>declared-but-not-read</em>).</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The contract is: inject, read, cite, respond. Every failure mode in this report is a deviation from one of these steps.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <h3>What the Harness Does NOT Check</h3>
    <p>The evaluation harness verifies the <em>procedural</em> chain (was the file read? was it cited?) but does <strong>not</strong> evaluate whether the rule&rsquo;s guidance was correctly applied in the response content. Assessing application quality would require semantic judgment and is outside the scope of this protocol compliance benchmark.</p>

    <h3>Why This Design?</h3>
    <p><strong>Verifiability.</strong> Each step produces an observable artifact. The harness doesn&rsquo;t need to understand <em>what</em> the model read; it only needs to confirm <em>that</em> it read.</p>
    <p><strong>Freshness guarantee.</strong> Without the read requirement, a model could &ldquo;comply&rdquo; using stale parametric knowledge. The <code>read_file</code> call forces the current version into context.</p>
    <p><strong>Deterministic injection.</strong> The hook-based matching uses the same deterministic code path in both production and evaluation, ensuring the benchmark tests exactly what ships.</p>

    <h3>Rules vs. Infrastructure</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h4>Rules (read + cited by model)</h4>
        <p>Files under <code>rules/</code> with numeric prefixes (e.g., <code>100-snowflake-core.md</code>). These are the domain-specific guidance documents that models must read and cite in their PRE-FLIGHT header.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Infrastructure (injected automatically)</h4>
        <p>The micro-kernel and candidate list are injected by the hook; the model does not read these via tool calls. They provide the behavioral contract and discovery hints, but are not themselves cited as rules.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Key Terminology</h3>
    <details>
      <summary>Glossary of terms used in this report</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fixture:</strong> A synthetic user prompt designed to test specific rule-loading paths, with an explicit expected rule set.</li>
        <li><strong>Micro-kernel:</strong> A compressed (~500 token) foundation injected on every prompt, defining mandatory behaviors and the rule-loading contract.</li>
        <li><strong>Candidate list:</strong> A ranked set of rule files whose keyword/extension/path triggers matched the current prompt, injected alongside the micro-kernel.</li>
        <li><strong>PRE-FLIGHT header:</strong> The structured block in the model&rsquo;s response declaring which rules were read and cited.</li>
        <li><strong>Tool trace:</strong> The complete sequence of tool calls made by the model during a single run: the ground truth for compliance auditing.</li>
        <li><strong>Parametric memory:</strong> Knowledge encoded in the model&rsquo;s weights during training, which may be stale relative to current rule content.</li>
        <li><strong>Stochastic instability:</strong> Identical inputs producing different compliance outcomes across runs due to sampling variance.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>
    </div><!-- end sub-panel protocol-overview -->

