    <!-- ===== METHODOLOGY SECTION (merged from Architecture tab) ===== -->
    <h2 style="margin-top:0;">Methodology: How the Benchmark Works</h2>

    <p>The evaluation benchmark exists to answer one operational question: <em>is the protocol being followed?</em> It is invoked via the <code>ai-rules rule-loader eval</code> CLI command, which dispatches fixture runs, resolves model connections via Snowflake Cortex, controls concurrency, and streams structured results to disk.</p>

    <p>The current architecture, a deterministic shell hook that injects matched rules into context before the model responds, exists as a direct consequence of these evaluation runs. An earlier incarnation relied on a <code>RULES_INDEX.md</code> manifest and an <code>AGENTS.md</code> instruction file that told models to discover and load matching rules on their own. The matching logic was conceptually identical, but the <em>discovery</em> step was left entirely to the model. Evaluation results revealed a sharply fragmented picture: instruction-following accuracy varied wildly across models when discovery was model-driven, producing unreliable compliance even when the underlying rules were clear and unambiguous.</p>
    <p>Moving the matching logic into a hook, which invokes the rule-loader skill automatically on every prompt, eliminated this variance for compliant models and reduced the evaluation to a narrower, more actionable question: given that the correct rules are already surfaced, does the model follow the read-then-cite contract? Users who prefer not to install the hook can still invoke the rule-loader skill manually to achieve the same result.</p>

    <h3>A Single Benchmark Run</h3>

    <p>When the eval is invoked, the harness executes each test fixture against the target model one or more times (<code>--runs</code>). Each fixture is a synthetic user prompt constructed to trigger a specific set of rule-loading paths. The fixture definition includes the expected rules, any rules that must <em>not</em> be loaded, and dependency relationships between rules.</p>

    <p>The runner dispatches each fixture to the model as a conversational turn with the micro-kernel and matched candidate list injected as the system context (identical to the production hook path). It captures the full tool-call trace across all turns until the model produces a final response. That response and trace are then passed to the scoring engine, which checks compliance across several dimensions before writing results to disk.</p>

    <h3>Test Fixtures</h3>

    <p>Fixtures are the fundamental unit of measurement. Each fixture specifies the prompt text, the set of rules the model <em>must</em> load (<code>required</code>), rules it must <em>not</em> load (<code>forbidden</code>), and dependency relationships between rules. Fixtures are organized into three complexity tiers:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Simple:</strong> Single-domain prompts that should trigger exactly one or two rules. Low ambiguity, high determinism.</li>
      <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Multi-domain prompts where the correct rule set is non-obvious. Tests classification accuracy without over-loading.</li>
      <li><strong>Complex:</strong> Multi-step tasks spanning several domains with dependent rules, forbidden exclusions, and structured output requirements. Most closely reflect real-world agentic workloads.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Each fixture is run multiple times per model to distinguish stable compliance from lucky passes. A model that passes a fixture only partially across runs is flagged as stochastically unstable: the rule loading is sensitive to sampling variance, which is a reliability risk even if the aggregate pass rate looks acceptable.</p>

    <h3>Scoring Dimensions</h3>
    <p>The scoring engine checks each fixture-run against multiple dimensions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Required rules present:</strong> Were all expected rules read via <code>read_file</code> and cited?</li>
      <li><strong>Forbidden rules absent:</strong> Were rules explicitly excluded by the fixture avoided?</li>
      <li><strong>Dependencies satisfied:</strong> If rule A was loaded, was its declared dependency B also loaded?</li>
      <li><strong>Cited-without-read detection (FM-1):</strong> Cross-references PRE-FLIGHT citations against the tool trace to detect fabricated claims.</li>
      <li><strong>Citation drift (FM-2):</strong> Validates declared metadata (line counts, versions) against actual file properties.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Output Artifacts</h3>

    <p>Every invocation streams results to a timestamped run directory under <code>results/</code>. Artifacts are written incrementally as each fixture completes, so partial runs are always recoverable:</p>

    <dl style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr; gap:0.4rem 1.2rem; font-size:0.9rem;">
      <dt><code>manifest.json</code></dt>
      <dd>Run-level metadata: model, runs, fixture count, concurrency, git SHA, timestamps, per-pass status index.</dd>

      <dt><code>run-NN/fixtures/&lt;id&gt;.json</code></dt>
      <dd>Primary scoring artifact: match result, signal report (FM signals), citation drifts, token counts, duration, final response text.</dd>

      <dt><code>run-NN/fixtures/&lt;id&gt;.transcript.jsonl</code></dt>
      <dd>Full tool-call trace as newline-delimited JSON. Ground truth for FM-1 detection.</dd>

      <dt><code>summary.json</code></dt>
      <dd>Aggregate pass rates consumed by the report generator to produce this document.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <strong>Scope of this benchmark</strong>
      <ul style="margin:0.5rem 0 0 1rem; padding:0;">
        <li>Measures procedural compliance (read + cite) only, not response quality or rule application correctness.</li>
        <li>Uses synthetic fixtures, not real user prompts. Real-world compliance may differ.</li>
        <li>Models are accessed via a single API provider (Snowflake Cortex). Provider-specific routing or caching could affect results.</li>
        <li>No cross-benchmark correlation data exists to compare these results against general reasoning or coding benchmarks.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
